+# Multiset resolving sets from a hidden parity cycle
+
+> **Build status:** the generator and local gates G1--G8 pass, but this result is
+> not yet releasable. All three required OpenRouter runs aborted at the account
+> layer with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before a model produced
+> a scored attempt. G9 therefore remains failed/unrun; the script-owned error
+> transcripts are retained as infrastructure evidence, not hardness evidence.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B -- no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT carried through the paper's graph reduction |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple / Boolean gadget choices, encoded as fixed-width hexadecimal |
+| Intended intuition | reduction recognition: recover parity quartets and their hidden cycle |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | Section 3, Theorem 3.1 and Claims 1--3; paper-central 3-SAT-to-Multiset-Dimension construction |
+
+## What the family is
+
+[Hakanen and Yero, *Complexity and Equivalency of Multiset Dimension and
+ID-colorings* (arXiv:2303.06986)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.06986) defines the
+multiset representation of a vertex as the unordered multiset of its distances
+to a chosen vertex set. The task is to find a canonical set whose representations
+are pairwise distinct in the connected graph built by the paper's variable and
+clause gadgets.
+
+Generation is inverse. It samples an assignment, evaluates the cyclic equations
+`x_i XOR x_(i+1) XOR x_(i+2) = b_i` in a hidden variable order, expands each
+equation into its four exact 3-CNF clauses, and carries the sampled assignment to
+Claim 2's set: one `e` vertex and one truth vertex per variable gadget, plus one
+`g` vertex per clause gadget. It never runs SAT or elimination. The hexadecimal
+answer selects the truth vertices and therefore denotes the full concrete graph
+vertex set.
+
+Checking is exact. A false clause makes the complete distance multisets of
+`c_j^1` and `c_j^3` equal; when all clauses are true, Claim 2's displayed distance
+identities and the checked distinct path lengths distinguish all other pairs.
+`verify` executes this condition for every clause and accepts every satisfying
+canonical witness, without reading `inst["answer"]`. As a transcription audit,
+the self-test also materializes each 1,890-vertex demo graph and recomputes every
+distance multiset by BFS.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+Theorem 3.1 proves the general decision problem NP-complete, but that worst-case
+result says nothing about this structured distribution. This distribution has a
+known efficient solution: group the four clauses on every triple and use
+Gauss--Jordan elimination over GF(2), `O(n^3)` under the counted scalar model.
+At shipping `n=251`, eight seeds averaged **633,145 scalar operations and
+0.008--0.05 seconds** across repeated local runs. Conventional DPLL with unit
+propagation also solved 8/8, averaging 347,266 literal inspections, 4.625 nodes
+and 0.05--0.19 seconds. These are successful
+reference algorithms, not failed attacks.
+
+The compact route notices that pairs occurring in two parity triples trace a
+hidden cycle. Consecutive equations yield a step-three recurrence, and a packed
+parallel-prefix XOR reconstructs all bits in **108 counted wide-word operations**.
+The benchmark gap is between that structural route and mechanically processing
+roughly one thousand shuffled clause gadgets without tools.
+
+The paper's explicitly easy construction regimes were avoided: Proposition 4.2
+and Theorem 4.4 display four-vertex resolving sets for king grids, while Theorem
+5.1/Corollary 5.3 gives a whole-copy witness for `G strong-product K_2` when `G`
+is multiset distance irregular. Those would test formula recall, not recovery.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The complete `demo`, seed 7, is:
+
+```text
+Find a multiset resolving set in the graph defined below.
+
+Exact definition.
+The graph is simple, undirected and connected. For vertices u,v, d(u,v)
+is the number of edges in a shortest u-v path. For a vertex set W, the
+multiset representation of u is the unordered multiset {{d(u,w): w in W}},
+including distance 0 when u is in W. The set W is multiset resolving when
+these multisets are different for every two distinct graph vertices.
+
+Variable gadgets.
+There are n=5 gadgets. A gadget X with displayed identifier x and
+path length t has vertices T_x,F_x,a_x^1,a_x^2,b_x^1,b_x^2,
+d_x^1,...,d_x^t,e_x^1,e_x^2. Its edges are:
+  a_x^1--b_x^1, a_x^2--b_x^2;
+  T_x--a_x^1, T_x--a_x^2, F_x--b_x^1, F_x--b_x^2;
+  T_x--d_x^1, F_x--d_x^1, d_x^h--d_x^(h+1) for 1<=h<t;
+  d_x^t--e_x^1 and d_x^t--e_x^2.
+The gadgets in increasing t order are numbered r=0,...,n-1:
+  r=000: x=150631  t=10
+  r=001: x=175954  t=15
+  r=002: x=961168  t=20
+  r=003: x=661913  t=25
+  r=004: x=198702  t=30
+
+Clause gadgets.
+There are M=20 clauses. Clause C with identifier q, path
+length s and three distinct signed variable identifiers has vertices
+c_q^1,c_q^2,c_q^3,f_q^1,...,f_q^s,g_q^1,g_q^2. Its internal edges are
+  c_q^1--c_q^2--c_q^3; c_q^2--f_q^1;
+  f_q^h--f_q^(h+1) for 1<=h<s; f_q^s--g_q^1 and f_q^s--g_q^2.
+For every clause q, add c_q^1--T_x and c_q^1--F_x for every variable x.
+Also connect c_q^3 to both T_x,F_x when x is absent from the clause; connect
+it only to F_x when +x occurs; and only to T_x when -x occurs.
+There are no edges other than those just specified. A token +x is true when
+x's bit is 1, and -x is true when that bit is 0. All bounds are inclusive.
+The clauses, sorted by their structurally distinguishing path length s, are:
+  q=708064  s=35: +175954 -198702 +661913
+  q=935601  s=40: +198702 -175954 +661913
+  q=578365  s=45: +198702 +175954 -661913
+  q=172103  s=50: -661913 -198702 -175954
+  q=980770  s=55: +150631 +661913 +198702
+  q=198142  s=60: -198702 +150631 -661913
+  q=383051  s=65: -150631 +198702 -661913
+  q=597128  s=70: -150631 +661913 -198702
+  q=830901  s=75: -961168 +150631 -198702
+  q=796414  s=80: +961168 -150631 -198702
+  q=168157  s=85: -961168 +198702 -150631
+  q=163616  s=90: +961168 +150631 +198702
+  q=866676  s=95: +961168 -175954 -150631
+  q=835567  s=100: -961168 +150631 -175954
+  q=424646  s=105: -150631 -961168 +175954
+  q=778563  s=110: +150631 +175954 +961168
+  q=706020  s=115: +175954 -661913 -961168
+  q=814328  s=120: +661913 +175954 +961168
+  q=961850  s=125: -661913 +961168 -175954
+  q=567288  s=130: +661913 -175954 -961168
+
+Required witness.
+Return one hexadecimal word w of exactly 2 digits and value below 2^5.
+Leading zeroes are required. Bit r of w (bit 0 is the least-significant bit)
+corresponds to the variable gadget on row r above. It defines the concrete set
+  W(w) = {e_x^1 for every variable x} union {g_q^1 for every clause q}
+         union {a_x^1 if x's bit is 1, otherwise b_x^1, for every x}.
+Thus W(w) has exactly 2n+M=30 vertices. Find any w for which
+W(w) is a multiset resolving set. Hexadecimal letter case is ignored on input.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as exactly the required
+2 hexadecimal digits, without a 0x prefix.
+Example format only: <answer>07</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>12</answer>`. `verify(inst, "12")` returns
+`(True, "ok")`; `verify(inst, "00")` returns `(False, "c_814328^1 and
+c_814328^3 have identical multiset-distance representations")`. A person can
+solve this smallest case by checking 32 masks or reconstructing its five-cycle.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | `n` | copy bound | Clause gadgets (typical) | Implicit graph vertices (seed 0) | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 5 | 1 | 20 | 1,890 | hand-solvable illustration; hardening skips it |
+| easy | 251 | 1 | 1,004 | 3,954,003 | intended shipping preset; oracle evidence blocked |
+| medium | 253 | 2 | about 1,500 | 7,670,124 | reserve escalation rung |
+| hard | 254 | 3 | about 2,000 | 12,640,127 | reserve escalation rung |
+
+`n` enlarges the Boolean search space while hexadecimal keeps the answer short;
+`copies` adds graph/clause crowding without changing the witness length. Beyond
+256 semantic answer atoms, `escalate` returns `"cap_bound"`.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | pass: 12/12 symbolic checks; 3/3 full demo BFS expansions; JSON round-trip |
+| G2 | pass: 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass: 63-digit answer recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass: 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; language size `2^251` |
+| G5 | pass: exactly one constructed solution; demo enumeration also finds one; strongest failed attack used at most 504,008 trial evaluations and 1.6--9.0 s over eight seeds in repeated runs |
+| G6 | pass: five attacks each 0/8; Gaussian, DPLL and compact reference routes each 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass: doubled `n=502` instance built and verified (15,783,131 implicit vertices) |
+| G8 | pass: 40/40 composed relabellings invariant, 40 carried witnesses valid, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **fail/unrun:** size caps pass, but no scored hinted-oracle verdict exists |
+
+Exact timing fields live in `selftest_report.json` and vary slightly per run.
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+The bare harness opened the shipping `easy` rung, but the account rejected all
+four initial/redraw calls before inference. Errors do not count as model failures,
+so there is no hardness verdict.
+
+| Mode | Preset | Scored attempts | Calls retained | Result |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 | 4 | HTTP 403 account total-limit error |
+| structural hint | easy | 0 | 4 | HTTP 403 account total-limit error |
+| placebo hint | easy | 0 | 4 | HTTP 403 account total-limit error |
+
+## G9 arms
+
+| Arm | Solved / valid attempts | Status |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | infrastructure abort |
+| hinted | 0/0 | infrastructure abort; polarity-flipped gate not established |
+| placebo | 0/0 | infrastructure abort |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is undefined with no valid attempts; the report stores numeric
+placeholder `0.0` and makes no inference about hint benefit. The hint names only
+the parity-quartet/hidden-cycle invariant. The shipping answer is 65 JSON
+characters, approximately 17 tokens and 251 semantic atoms; the intended route
+uses 108 exact wide-word operations. Those size/effort caps pass.
+
+## How to use it
+
+```python
+import random
+import gen_2303_06986 as gen
+
+inst = gen.make_instance(seed=7, **gen.DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+question = gen.render(inst)
+candidate = gen.parse_answer("Reasoning... <answer>12</answer>")
+assert gen.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+assert gen.random_candidate(inst, random.Random(1)) is not None
+```
+
+Once OpenRouter quota is restored and all oracle gates pass, emit from the
+repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2303.06986 20 easy
+```
+
+The module is standard-library-only and performs no file I/O, network access or
+printing at import.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is deliberately Track B. A sandbox, XOR-aware SAT solver or GF(2) linear
+  algebra makes it easy; no average-case or cryptographic hardness is claimed.
+- `0/200,000` samples uniformly from canonical one-bit-per-variable choices. It
+  does not estimate density among arbitrary subsets of the roughly four million
+  graph vertices. The canonical language is explicit in the problem statement.
+- The scalable verifier uses the exact equivalence proved by Claims 1--3 rather
+  than expanding millions of pendant-path vertices. The independent BFS audit
+  covers demo instances, not the shipping graph.
+- The panel includes incidence, sign-frequency, one-bit greedy, 8,192 random
+  restarts and a public-order recurrence. It does not include an industrial CDCL
+  solver; successful DPLL and Gaussian references already demonstrate tool ease.
+- The shipping prompt is about 53 KB because it displays every clause. Failure
+  caused solely by reading/transcription burden would be less informative than
+  failure to recognize the hidden parity structure; the missing oracle arms are
+  needed to diagnose this.
+- Canonicalization is exact for this construction's relevant relabellings because
+  unique pendant lengths rank every gadget. It is not a general graph-isomorphism
+  algorithm outside this family.
