\begin{remark}
	We have $\dim_{ms} (P_2 \boxtimes P_2) = \dim_{ms} (P_3 \boxtimes P_3) = \infty$, since both of these graphs are of diameter at most 2, and such graphs have no multiset resolving sets~\cite{rino-2017}. As for the case $n=4$, the set $\{(1,1),(2,1),(4,1),(1,3),(2,3),(2,4)\}$ is a multiset resolving set of $P_4 \boxtimes P_4$. We have checked with an exhaustive computer search that no smaller multiset resolving sets exist for this graph, and thus $\dim_{ms} (P_4 \boxtimes P_4) = 6$
\end{remark}


We begin our exposition of the larger king grids with the two smallest cases, and further on proceed with the general case.\vspace*{-1mm}
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:P5P5}
We have $\dim_{ms} (P_5 \boxtimes P_5) = \dim_{ms} (P_6 \boxtimes P_6)= 4$.\vspace*{-2mm}
\end{proposition}


